{"question": "A 28-year-old primigravid woman at 39 weeks gestation is admitted to the hospital in active labor. On examination, the cervix is 100% effaced and 10 cm dilated. After 5 minutes of pushing, there is a prolonged deceleration of the fetal heart rate to 90/min. A decision to perform an episiotomy is made to expedite vaginal delivery. The anesthesiologist locates the ischial spines by palpating the posterolateral vaginal sidewall and administers an anesthetic. Three minutes later, pinching the posterior vulva does not provoke pain. The anesthetized nerve most likely also supplies which of the following structures?", "options": {"A": "Obturator externus muscle", "B": "Skin of the lateral thigh", "C": "External anal sphincter", "D": "Skin of the mons pubis", "E": "Detrusor muscle"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Obstetrics", "Anesthesiology", "Gynecology", "Neonatology", "Emergency Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Anesthesiology", "Obstetrics"], "question_analyses": {"Obstetrics": "The scenario describes a woman in active labor at term gestation who is experiencing a prolonged deceleration of the fetal heart rate (FHR) during the second stage of labor. The deceleration to 90 bpm is concerning as it indicates a significant drop in the fetal heart rate, which can be a sign of fetal distress. The decision to perform an episiotomy is made to expedite the delivery, which is a common intervention in such scenarios to reduce the duration of labor and potentially mitigate the risk of further fetal compromise. The anesthesiologist is administering a local anesthetic to the area around the ischial spines, which are bony prominences located on either side of the midline of the pelvis. The anesthetic is administered by locating the ischial spines through palpation of the posterolateral vaginal sidewall. After the administration of the anesthetic, the patient does not feel pain when the posterior vulva is pinched, indicating that the anesthe